  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nTowards the end of the work week, deterministic guidance shows \nfalling 500mb heights just to our west and shortwaves coming off\nof the Rockies from a trough over the Western Canadian coast \ninto the Pacific NW. A high at the surface over the northern \nGreat Lakes and into southern Ontario will result in \nsoutheasterly flow at the surface bringing moisture from the \nGulf into eastern KS. With synoptic scale lift and moisture \navailable at the surface we could see some rain in our area \nThursday night into Friday but deterministic and ensemble \nguidance keeps most of the rain off to our west until Friday \nmorning with about a 15-30% chance of rain Thursday night in NW \nMO gradually increasing to a 50-60% chance Friday morning for NW\nMO and 30-50% chance of rain for the rest of the CWA. It is \nunlikely that we'll see any thunderstorms with this first round \nof showers as instability and shear looks to be pretty weak.\n \nThe greater chance for rain moves into our area this weekend, \nespecially Saturday night into Sunday. Guidance shows 500mb \nheights continuing to fall as we move into more zonal flow and \nthe closed low off the east coast starts to lift further north. \nThe surface high over the Great Lakes continues to bring \nsoutherly flow and moisture from the Gulf into our area \nsupporting rain chances through the weekend. We could hear some \nrumbles of thunder with the rain on Saturday night with guidance\nshowing weak instability on the western edge of our CWA but \notherwise we shouldn't see any severe weather as deterministic \nguidance keeps the greatest instability outside of the CWA. We \ncould potentially see some warmer temperatures this weekend and \nthe beginning of next week with a 70-80% chance of highs above \n85, especially south of I-70.",
